      "claim": "The captured militia member does not qualify as a prisoner of war because he failed to satisfy the requirement of a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance under Article 4(2)(b) of the Third Geneva Convention.",
      "reasoning": "Article 4(2) limits POW status for members of other militias to those who meet four conditions, one of which is \"that of having a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance\". The facts state that the militia member was wearing ordinary civilian clothing with no insignia or other distinctive sign at the time of capture. Because this condition was not met at the moment he fell into the power of the enemy, the statutory prerequisite for POW status is absent, and the individual cannot be classified as a prisoner of war.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iii",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "that of having a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The presumption of POW status in Additional Protocol I art.45(1) is rebutted by the clear failure to meet the distinctive‑sign requirement, allowing Ardovia to lawfully treat the detainee as a civilian.",
      "reasoning": "Art.45(1) creates a presumption that a person who takes part in hostilities and is captured is a POW, but it also provides that \"Should any doubt arise as to whether any such person is entitled to the status of prisoner of war, he shall continue to have such status ... until such time as his status has been determined by a competent tribunal.\" The lack of a distinctive sign creates a factual doubt about entitlement, triggering the rebuttal mechanism. Consequently, Ardovia may lawfully deny POW status pending a competent determination, which in this case is satisfied by the clear statutory breach identified in Argument 1.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "45(1)",
          "quoted_text": "A person who takes part in hostilities and falls into the power of an adverse Party shall be presumed to be a prisoner of war, ... Should any doubt arise as to whether any such person is entitled to the status of prisoner of war, he shall continue to have such status ... until such time as his status has been determined by a competent tribunal."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Because the militia member did not display a distinctive sign, he remains a civilian under international humanitarian law and is therefore entitled only to the protections of Common Article 3, not to POW status.",
      "reasoning": "The definition of combatants in IHL requires that they distinguish themselves from the civilian population. The absence of any fixed distinctive sign at the time of capture means the individual cannot be classified as a combatant. Under Article 4(2) this exclusion precludes POW status, leaving the individual subject only to the minimum guarantees afforded to persons who are not combatants, namely the humane treatment standards of Common Article 3. Thus Ardovia’s refusal to accord POW status is consistent with the treaty framework.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iii",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "that of having a fixed distinctive sign recognizable at a distance;"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
